#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111453} :activity/id #uuid "c970a902-f20b-5036-ab65-b55e2fe752d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048634 :topic/id #uuid "b055f284-c392-536d-9736-e7678bfb5ad9" :topic/math-academy-id 1470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111916 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate probabilities for normally distributed random variables $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ when $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are both *known.* \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean $\\mu$ or the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a normally distributed random variable when given a specific probability.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a normally distributed random variable $X$ with *unknown* mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$ and *known* variance ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}}^2 = {\\color{red}2}^2.$ We write this as follows:\n\n$$\nX \\sim N({\\color{blue}\\mu}, {\\color{red}2}^2)\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 2.1) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nWe can use this additional information about $X$ to calculate the mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}.$ \n\nFirst, we transform to a standard normal random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nApplying the $z$-scoring transformation to our probability statement, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.1) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}} = {\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nP\\left(Z \\leq \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{\\color{red}{2}}\\right) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $P(Z\\leq z) = \\Phi(z),$ where $\\Phi$ is the cumulative distribution function of $Z,$ we can write the above statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\\right)   = 0.7088 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.55) = 0.7088 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088) = 0.55.\n$$\n\nEquating our two expressions for $\\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088),$ we can now solve for $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.1-\\mu }{2} & =0.55 \\\\ 2.1-\\mu  & =1.1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a61536c-c1a8-53d1-a0b6-46caba6fec29" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8989 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6fa2f7f1-e420-537d-bc2b-81367567a256" :step/math-academy-id 118366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110337 :question/id #uuid "9662033b-2a3a-5d80-91ad-ed542898b80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048615}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360512}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"} :step/id #uuid "d6ecea93-68d9-5f9e-8593-fdb0566ffc45" :step/math-academy-id 118367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110565 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110338 :question/id #uuid "35cc4c4c-79fb-5ae2-94e0-79f007af832b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14545"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360518}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"} :step/id #uuid "3bf2d118-febb-52bb-99a0-706e53dac37b" :step/math-academy-id 118370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110566 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110339 :question/id #uuid "2a8633b4-f937-57e3-899d-c3176f07a48f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14546"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "b06d90dc-2c1d-585d-a385-9e6d4338a8ae" :step/math-academy-id 118373 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111917 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma })=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma })=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <        X <       2) = P(-1 - 3 <   X <   -1 + 3) \n\\]\n\nSo, the axis of symmetry for our distribution is [math]x=-1,[/math] as shown below. \n\nUsing its symmetry, we get\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <   X <   2) = 2 P(-1 <  X <   2),\n\\]\n\nand find the cumulative probability as follows:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(-4 \\lt X \\lt  2)\t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\t2 P(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\tP(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - P(X \\lt -1) \t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - 0.5 \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\leq 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \n\\end{align*}\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by [math]z[/math]-scoring:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(X \\leq 2)\t\t\t\t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP \\left( Z \\leq \\dfrac{2 - (-1)}{\\sigma} \\right)\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\Phi \\left( \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \\right)\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \t\t\t\t\t&= \\Phi^{-1} (0.9357 ) \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\n\n-\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9207 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9332 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9345 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & \\approx 1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a748c912-a01f-5524-83df-8f7ffeb40112" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8990 :tutorial/title "Using Symmetry Properties"} :step/id #uuid "e024483a-aee7-570d-b430-7d0d9873573d" :step/math-academy-id 118376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110567 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110340 :question/id #uuid "5eb435ff-ef12-5f18-89d6-4ac163fae344" :question/math-academy-id "e-14548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317203}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"} :step/id #uuid "734444df-a80e-5540-b091-e90d3a989d01" :step/math-academy-id 118377}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110839}]}
